to document the criteria for this so that the intake team can handle this case. So what we do

00:59:37.920 --> 00:59:42.640
is, and sometimes it takes one, two, or three sessions where we sit down with the attorney

00:59:42.640 --> 00:59:47.760
and we truly understand all of the information that they need. And now that you have this

00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:53.520
information, what do you consider a weak case? What do you consider a really strong case? And

00:59:53.520 --> 00:59:59.760
why? So we just dig and dig and we probe and we probe and then we document the criteria.

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:02.880
We're the intake team so that they have a roadmap of,

01:00:02.880 --> 01:00:05.640
this is a clear signup, we might reject it later,

01:00:05.640 --> 01:00:07.640
but this is one that they will feel really good about

01:00:07.640 --> 01:00:09.760
if you don't let pass, right?

01:00:09.760 --> 01:00:11.560
And this is what they consider a weak case.

01:00:11.560 --> 01:00:13.920
It's okay for you to bring in an attorney

01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:16.980
just in case there's maybe some nuances

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:18.600
that they'll be able to discover,

01:00:18.600 --> 01:00:21.040
but you wanna get as close as possible.

01:00:21.040 --> 01:00:22.680
And then of course you have your med mals.

01:00:22.680 --> 01:00:25.360
If I were a law firm, and you know you're gonna get

01:00:25.360 --> 01:00:29.780
X amount, if you're lucky, of seven, eight-figure cases,

01:00:29.780 --> 01:00:33.180
if it's a catastrophic truck accident

01:00:33.180 --> 01:00:34.620
and you know the trucking company,

01:00:34.620 --> 01:00:36.540
you know that there's gonna already be

01:00:36.540 --> 01:00:39.220
a minimum policy of millions,

01:00:39.220 --> 01:00:43.020
is it like, hey, we have a really fucking hot lead?

01:00:43.020 --> 01:00:46.420
I need an attorney that can ideally also sign up cases.

01:00:46.420 --> 01:00:48.260
So I think that that's another,

01:00:48.260 --> 01:00:49.580
I don't want anyone to get mad at me,

01:00:49.580 --> 01:00:52.360
I know all of you think you're the best at signing up cases,

01:00:52.360 --> 01:00:53.900
but I do think there are lawyers

01:00:53.900 --> 01:00:56.700
that are better at it than others.

01:00:56.700 --> 01:00:59.020
I've even heard some that I think the lawyers

01:00:59.060 --> 01:01:00.980
can be kind of rude and condescending,

01:01:00.980 --> 01:01:04.460
and I'm like struggling because-

01:01:04.460 --> 01:01:07.180
They're busy, which is why you should have an intake team.

01:01:07.180 --> 01:01:09.380
Okay, yes, but now let's,

01:01:09.380 --> 01:01:11.220
and typically I hear this when people

01:01:11.220 --> 01:01:13.900
don't actually have a case, right, believe it or not,

01:01:13.900 --> 01:01:15.380
is when I hear more, and I'm like,

01:01:15.380 --> 01:01:17.220
oh, that was the chance for a review,

01:01:17.220 --> 01:01:18.820
explain to them why they don't have a case,

01:01:18.820 --> 01:01:21.240
be friendly, get me that review.

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:24.300
But that's a whole conversation for another day.

01:01:24.300 --> 01:01:26.140
Is there a point where it's like,

01:01:27.140 --> 01:01:32.140
if this magic criteria hits, we involve a lawyer?

01:01:33.420 --> 01:01:35.300
Or do you think, no, the intake team

01:01:35.300 --> 01:01:36.540
should be the best at closing it,

01:01:36.540 --> 01:01:39.300
they should have examples of results

01:01:39.300 --> 01:01:41.060
of cases that have been gone to trial.

01:01:41.060 --> 01:01:43.060
Should they explain, if there's a trial firm,

01:01:43.060 --> 01:01:46.520
should they explain the difference between a settlement firm

01:01:46.520 --> 01:01:50.260
or a pre-litigation firm and a firm

01:01:50.260 --> 01:01:51.340
that actually goes to trial?

01:01:51.340 --> 01:01:53.300
Because this is something I hear a lot from lawyers, right?

01:01:53.460 --> 01:01:55.940
We actually go to trial and they're so proud of it.

01:01:55.940 --> 01:01:58.860
And I'm like, how proud you are,

01:01:58.860 --> 01:02:01.380
I wanna hear your intake be that proud.

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:03.620
And explain to the average consumer,

01:02:03.620 --> 01:02:04.980
they don't understand the difference

01:02:04.980 --> 01:02:08.180
between you and a big advertiser

01:02:08.180 --> 01:02:10.820
that you claim doesn't go to trial.

01:02:10.820 --> 01:02:13.140
Because I'm not trying to put down big advertisers either.

01:02:13.140 --> 01:02:17.100
I think that's a super hard job, right?

01:02:17.100 --> 01:02:19.300
I'm all about marketing, so kudos to them.

01:02:19.300 --> 01:02:22.500
But what I hear from the trial lawyer is,

01:02:22.540 --> 01:02:24.460
all these people ranking, or not all,

01:02:24.460 --> 01:02:27.740
but A, B, C, and D, and this big,

01:02:27.740 --> 01:02:28.780
they don't go to trial.

01:02:28.780 --> 01:02:31.100
Okay, is that passion coming through?

01:02:31.100 --> 01:02:32.940
And I know where I'm like, I'm always like-

01:02:32.940 --> 01:02:34.620
No, I'm keeping up with the questions.

01:02:34.620 --> 01:02:37.340
Well, it's all kind of flows, right?

01:02:37.340 --> 01:02:38.620
So let's just go back.

01:02:38.620 --> 01:02:40.860
Is there a point when the lawyer has to be brought back in?

01:02:40.860 --> 01:02:44.700
So we establish what we call an escalation policy.

01:02:44.700 --> 01:02:49.140
So all prospects, all potential injured people

01:02:49.140 --> 01:02:50.860
should be given the same level of service,

01:02:50.860 --> 01:02:53.140
but there are some that we escalate,

01:02:53.140 --> 01:02:55.620
which means that that intake specialist

01:02:55.620 --> 01:02:57.780
is going to actually cover their same call path

01:02:57.780 --> 01:03:00.820
as they would with any other viable claim,

01:03:00.820 --> 01:03:03.300
which actually includes things like

01:03:03.300 --> 01:03:04.900
establishing the value propositions

01:03:04.900 --> 01:03:08.060
of the fact that perhaps we are trial attorneys, right?

01:03:08.060 --> 01:03:09.180
And that we have this experience,

01:03:09.180 --> 01:03:10.500
or maybe we have attorneys

01:03:10.500 --> 01:03:11.980
that have practiced on the defense side

01:03:11.980 --> 01:03:13.540
and they understand, right?

01:03:13.540 --> 01:03:15.100
And then they have that intel knowledge.

01:03:15.100 --> 01:03:16.460
All of that has already been sprinkled

01:03:16.460 --> 01:03:17.500
throughout the call, right?

01:03:17.500 --> 01:03:20.300
If the intake specialist is applying that.

01:03:20.620 --> 01:03:22.260
Which I would argue is sales.

01:03:22.260 --> 01:03:23.740
Yes.

01:03:23.740 --> 01:03:26.020
So if there is a case where there is like

01:03:26.020 --> 01:03:29.020
an overnight hospitalization involved, right?

01:03:29.020 --> 01:03:31.660
Or a TBI injury, not only,

01:03:31.660 --> 01:03:34.700
so the conversion or the ask is different.

01:03:34.700 --> 01:03:36.260
It's when can we come to you?

01:03:36.260 --> 01:03:37.860
When can we come to the hospital?

01:03:37.860 --> 01:03:42.700
So they're still expected to have that same level of gusto

01:03:42.700 --> 01:03:44.620
when it comes to making the close,

01:03:44.620 --> 01:03:46.900
but the close means when's the appointment,

01:03:46.900 --> 01:03:48.660
when's visiting schedule.

01:03:49.460 --> 01:03:52.180
They're still explaining how everything works,

01:03:52.180 --> 01:03:55.060
but they're giving them VIP service.

01:03:55.060 --> 01:03:57.860
So they're still expected to follow that.

01:03:57.860 --> 01:04:00.220
They're not going to stop what they're doing,

01:04:00.220 --> 01:04:01.820
look for an attorney, maybe they're busy

01:04:01.820 --> 01:04:03.540
and then have to call that person back.

01:04:03.540 --> 01:04:05.940
They're just sending it off.

01:04:05.940 --> 01:04:08.620
It's in motion, there's nothing to think about,

01:04:08.620 --> 01:04:11.020
put it on the attorney's calendar and get it scheduled.

01:04:11.020 --> 01:04:13.580
And the attorneys all had that expectation.

01:04:13.580 --> 01:04:16.740
They would be more than happy on a weekend.

01:04:16.740 --> 01:04:17.580
Right.

01:04:17.580 --> 01:04:18.620
Oh, I'm sure.

01:04:19.260 --> 01:04:21.020
To attend that, just put it on their schedule.

01:04:21.020 --> 01:04:24.140
So we have an escalation policy

01:04:24.140 --> 01:04:26.780
that's about the level of urgency

01:04:26.780 --> 01:04:28.100
of when you can just sign it up

01:04:28.100 --> 01:04:31.060
versus sign it up, schedule an appointment

01:04:31.060 --> 01:04:33.700
and get the ball rolling immediately.

01:04:33.700 --> 01:04:34.540
Got it.

01:04:34.540 --> 01:04:36.100
And then now my second question,

01:04:36.100 --> 01:04:38.180
you've kind of already answered it is,

01:04:38.180 --> 01:04:40.980
do you have a very specific

01:04:40.980 --> 01:04:42.260
or do you recommend that law firms

01:04:42.260 --> 01:04:45.300
have very specific processes per type of case

01:04:45.300 --> 01:04:49.540
and like results and, you know,

01:04:49.540 --> 01:04:53.540
whether it's settlements or verdicts or expertise

01:04:53.540 --> 01:04:57.100
and really tailoring it to the type of case

01:04:57.100 --> 01:05:01.020
or type of, you know, accident or injury that occurred?

01:05:01.020 --> 01:05:01.860
Absolutely.

01:05:01.860 --> 01:05:05.900
So your intake process for every case type

01:05:05.900 --> 01:05:09.780
should be very tailored to that case type

01:05:09.780 --> 01:05:11.380
or that sub liability.

01:05:11.380 --> 01:05:13.060
It should be very tailored to that.

01:05:13.060 --> 01:05:15.780
It should include very granular questions

01:05:15.780 --> 01:05:16.940
while having control of the call

01:05:16.940 --> 01:05:19.140
but just being able to know when to pivot.

01:05:19.140 --> 01:05:21.020
Your intake team can learn these things.

01:05:21.020 --> 01:05:21.940
It's very possible.

01:05:21.940 --> 01:05:25.940
It just takes time to map this stuff out.

01:05:25.940 --> 01:05:28.620
When it comes to being able to share results,

01:05:28.620 --> 01:05:31.020
a lot of our clients have intake teams

01:05:31.020 --> 01:05:33.900
with team members that had pre-lit experience

01:05:33.900 --> 01:05:34.740
within their firm.

01:05:34.740 --> 01:05:35.980
So they have that advantage,

01:05:35.980 --> 01:05:37.660
but we've also worked with intake teams

01:05:37.660 --> 01:05:41.660
where maybe they were a little newer to the industry.

01:05:41.660 --> 01:05:46.060
So they need more resources like, you know,

01:05:46.060 --> 01:05:48.100
prior settlements, but we love that.

01:05:48.100 --> 01:05:53.100
We love when our firms can share maybe a recent verdict

01:05:53.360 --> 01:05:55.180
or a settlement with their team

01:05:55.180 --> 01:05:57.660
and give them an archive or a collection, right,

01:05:57.660 --> 01:05:58.860
of stories that they can share

01:05:58.860 --> 01:06:01.540
because they may not have a point of reference

01:06:01.540 --> 01:06:03.580
where they can relate to it personally,

01:06:03.580 --> 01:06:06.340
but they're able to share what the experience is like

01:06:06.340 --> 01:06:08.540
for someone that has gone through it as a client

01:06:08.540 --> 01:06:10.260
and how they came out on the other side

01:06:10.260 --> 01:06:12.060
to the best of their ability, right,

01:06:12.060 --> 01:06:13.860
to indemnify them at least,

01:06:13.860 --> 01:06:15.540
but they're able to come to them

01:06:15.540 --> 01:06:18.820
with a point of reference that humanizes someone else

01:06:18.820 --> 01:06:19.660
that's been through this.

01:06:19.660 --> 01:06:20.480
I know what you've been through.

01:06:20.480 --> 01:06:23.540
We actually had someone else that recently went through this

01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:27.020
and this is how we were able to take care of them.

01:06:27.020 --> 01:06:29.580
We always ask our clients

01:06:29.580 --> 01:06:31.380
or any firms that's listening to this,

01:06:31.380 --> 01:06:35.700
if you do share a success story with your team,

01:06:35.700 --> 01:06:38.460
do not send a Lawyer's Weekly article.

01:06:38.460 --> 01:06:43.460
Sit down with your team and practice storytelling.

01:06:44.340 --> 01:06:46.740
Tell them how this case played out,

01:06:46.740 --> 01:06:48.820
how it impacted their family.

01:06:48.820 --> 01:06:50.020
Humanize it for them.

01:06:50.020 --> 01:06:51.500
If they see that passion in you,

01:06:51.500 --> 01:06:54.980
it's just gonna feel that purpose that we spoke about

01:06:54.980 --> 01:06:55.980
at the beginning of this.

01:06:55.980 --> 01:06:59.580
So you don't have to do it every week,

01:06:59.580 --> 01:07:00.820
but just take the time,

01:07:00.820 --> 01:07:01.980
even if it's once a quarter,

01:07:01.980 --> 01:07:03.860
to just share one or two stories

01:07:03.860 --> 01:07:06.420
and be proud when you're sharing that

01:07:06.420 --> 01:07:07.940
and it's really going to give them

01:07:08.420 --> 01:07:10.020
the purpose that they need to truly believe

01:07:10.020 --> 01:07:13.100
that they have the most important job in the firm.

01:07:13.100 --> 01:07:14.700
So you know what's super interesting

01:07:14.700 --> 01:07:16.620
as I'm hearing you talk?

01:07:16.620 --> 01:07:19.300
I see so many correlations in the way

01:07:19.300 --> 01:07:24.300
that trial lawyers talk about speaking to a jury

01:07:25.320 --> 01:07:27.500
in the way that their intake team

01:07:27.500 --> 01:07:30.340
should be speaking to these potential clients.

01:07:30.340 --> 01:07:31.180
Do you see that?

01:07:31.180 --> 01:07:32.900
Of course, storytelling is storytelling.

01:07:32.900 --> 01:07:33.940
Isn't that crazy though?

01:07:33.940 --> 01:07:35.940
Because you would think that they would be

01:07:35.940 --> 01:07:37.740
the best at intake.

01:07:38.420 --> 01:07:41.260
Maybe we just need to make this like correlation for them.

01:07:41.260 --> 01:07:42.580
I swear, just one last thing,

01:07:42.580 --> 01:07:43.780
and then I will let you go

01:07:43.780 --> 01:07:44.940
because that you said it

01:07:44.940 --> 01:07:48.060
and I'm like, we need to talk about this.

01:07:48.060 --> 01:07:51.740
How do you teach an intake specialist

01:07:51.740 --> 01:07:56.740
to have and retain control of the call?

01:07:56.860 --> 01:08:00.020
And the reason that I asked this is because I think that

01:08:00.900 --> 01:08:04.980
their ability to remain in control of the conversation

01:08:04.980 --> 01:08:07.020
kind of taps into authority

01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:10.420
and that authority of like how they perceive the firm

01:08:10.420 --> 01:08:12.820
and how they perceive them to be experts.

01:08:12.820 --> 01:08:16.460
And I think oftentimes lawyers don't understand

01:08:16.460 --> 01:08:18.900
or don't want to acknowledge that

01:08:18.900 --> 01:08:21.300
because this is the first point of contact

01:08:21.300 --> 01:08:24.060
potentially typically it is, right?

01:08:24.060 --> 01:08:26.460
They are the firm in that moment.

01:08:26.460 --> 01:08:29.700
So if they are not friendly and empathetic

01:08:29.700 --> 01:08:34.700
and come across as an expert and passionate and with purpose,

01:08:35.060 --> 01:08:36.660
that means that their firm

01:08:36.660 --> 01:08:38.380
and everything that the lawyer stands for

01:08:38.380 --> 01:08:39.900
isn't coming across.

01:08:39.900 --> 01:08:40.900
Yeah, absolutely.

01:08:40.900 --> 01:08:43.300
They really are the first impression

01:08:43.300 --> 01:08:45.900
and they're going to be measured

01:08:45.900 --> 01:08:47.380
based on what the firm

01:08:47.380 --> 01:08:50.260
or what the potential client thought about the firm

01:08:50.260 --> 01:08:52.460
versus what they actually experienced, right?

01:08:52.460 --> 01:08:54.180
When they're on that call with the intake specialist.

01:08:54.180 --> 01:08:56.380
So how an intake specialist

01:08:56.380 --> 01:08:58.140
can maintain control of the call

01:08:58.140 --> 01:09:03.140
is to guide the caller or the prospect, right?

01:09:03.380 --> 01:09:05.500
Whatever format throughout the entire call

01:09:05.500 --> 01:09:08.540
that's setting an agenda, having empathy,

01:09:08.540 --> 01:09:10.860
but, and this is something I'll share.

01:09:10.860 --> 01:09:12.420
I hope everyone uses this

01:09:12.420 --> 01:09:14.500
but you can tailor it to your firm.

01:09:14.500 --> 01:09:15.980
So we teach intake specialists

01:09:15.980 --> 01:09:18.660
something called the Imago Dialogue.

01:09:18.660 --> 01:09:20.380
I could be mispronouncing that second

01:09:20.380 --> 01:09:22.580
or messing up that second part,

01:09:22.580 --> 01:09:24.260
but it's the Imago Dialogue.

01:09:24.260 --> 01:09:26.620
And it teaches the intake specialist

01:09:26.620 --> 01:09:29.620
to be able to validate, to empathize,

01:09:29.620 --> 01:09:31.260
but segue into a solution.

01:09:31.260 --> 01:09:33.820
Otherwise it just becomes one big therapeutic session

01:09:33.820 --> 01:09:35.660
of, oh my God, I can't believe that happened to you.

01:09:35.660 --> 01:09:36.540
That sounds terrible.

01:09:36.540 --> 01:09:38.180
I know, let me tell you more.

01:09:38.180 --> 01:09:40.740
And it just continues and it's in a one-hour call.

01:09:40.740 --> 01:09:43.380
So what we teach them is to empathize,

01:09:43.380 --> 01:09:44.980
you can validate,

01:09:44.980 --> 01:09:46.420
and then you have,

01:09:46.420 --> 01:09:48.380
we have a list of one-liners that they can use

01:09:48.380 --> 01:09:50.620
that segues them into the solution.

01:09:50.620 --> 01:09:52.420
So for instance, right?

01:09:52.420 --> 01:09:54.580
I could validate you and empathize with you

01:09:54.580 --> 01:09:55.580
and I'm so glad.

01:09:55.580 --> 01:09:56.420
Hold on.

01:09:56.420 --> 01:09:58.420
When you say validate and empathize,

01:09:58.420 --> 01:10:00.060
do you teach them to, like,

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:04.960
say what they said? What do you call it when you do that?

01:10:04.960 --> 01:10:05.800
To mirror. Mirror.

01:10:05.800 --> 01:10:07.120
It's part of the Imago dialogue.

01:10:07.120 --> 01:10:07.960
Oh, okay, okay.

01:10:07.960 --> 01:10:08.780
So that's what I'm going into.

01:10:08.780 --> 01:10:09.620
Yes.

01:10:09.620 --> 01:10:10.460
It's like very Chris Voss.

01:10:10.460 --> 01:10:11.280
Yes.

01:10:11.280 --> 01:10:12.360
If you've ever read Never Split the Difference,

01:10:12.360 --> 01:10:13.640
it's like you mirror.

01:10:13.640 --> 01:10:14.760
Mirror back to them.

01:10:14.760 --> 01:10:15.600
Right.

01:10:15.600 --> 01:10:16.520
Yes, you validate.

01:10:16.520 --> 01:10:17.480
Yes.

01:10:17.480 --> 01:10:19.200
You know who else teaches this?

01:10:19.200 --> 01:10:21.120
It's actually a lawyer that teaches this

01:10:21.120 --> 01:10:22.160
that's not in our space.

01:10:22.160 --> 01:10:24.820
It's like Shawn Callaway.

01:10:24.820 --> 01:10:25.660
Okay.

01:10:25.660 --> 01:10:28.480
And I believe he's blind, he's a lawyer,

01:10:28.480 --> 01:10:30.720
and he teaches something very similar.

01:10:30.720 --> 01:10:32.600
He says, I've never taken this course because I'm so busy,

01:10:32.600 --> 01:10:35.280
but I've wanted to because he literally talks

01:10:35.280 --> 01:10:37.360
about mirroring and validating.

01:10:37.360 --> 01:10:40.040
Yes, and it's so important because often

01:10:40.040 --> 01:10:43.660
when someone is repeating themselves over and over again,

01:10:43.660 --> 01:10:47.080
when we think about our own personal relationships

01:10:47.080 --> 01:10:49.020
where you might have had a disagreement

01:10:49.020 --> 01:10:51.080
and we're just kind of going in circles,

01:10:51.080 --> 01:10:52.840
it's because we think that the other person

01:10:52.840 --> 01:10:53.960
isn't listening to us.

01:10:53.960 --> 01:10:54.800
Right.

01:10:54.800 --> 01:10:55.620
Right?

01:10:55.620 --> 01:10:56.460
We don't think that they understand.

01:10:56.460 --> 01:10:57.520
You mirror, how do you validate?

01:10:57.520 --> 01:10:58.920
What's the difference between-

01:10:58.920 --> 01:10:59.760
Mirroring and validating.

01:10:59.760 --> 01:11:00.880
Yeah, why don't you explain what the difference is?

01:11:00.880 --> 01:11:04.480
Yeah, so if I am showing empathy, right?

01:11:04.480 --> 01:11:08.560
I am acknowledging that I can imagine how you feel.

01:11:08.560 --> 01:11:11.000
I can imagine that you feel frustrated

01:11:11.000 --> 01:11:14.280
dealing with this unexpected event in your life.

01:11:14.280 --> 01:11:16.120
Yeah, it sounds like this was very frustrating.

01:11:16.120 --> 01:11:20.000
Yes, so that is more of an understanding, right?

01:11:20.000 --> 01:11:21.680
We can put ourselves in their shoes.

01:11:21.680 --> 01:11:23.320
Whereas validating doesn't necessarily mean

01:11:23.320 --> 01:11:25.480
that you agree with them, right?

01:11:25.480 --> 01:11:27.400
It just means that it makes sense.

01:11:28.400 --> 01:11:30.200
And this will be a joke.

01:11:30.200 --> 01:11:32.600
It makes sense that you might be a little frustrated

01:11:32.600 --> 01:11:34.880
because you've left three voicemails to your paralegal

01:11:34.880 --> 01:11:36.920
and you haven't received a call back.

01:11:36.920 --> 01:11:38.600
It makes sense that you feel that way.

01:11:38.600 --> 01:11:40.440
I'm not agreeing with them.

01:11:40.440 --> 01:11:42.360
I'm not saying what you're saying is right,

01:11:42.360 --> 01:11:45.880
but it stops that call from becoming an unhinged call

01:11:45.880 --> 01:11:50.640
of projecting and a very upset caller on the other line.

01:11:50.640 --> 01:11:54.520
So by just letting them know that based on what you believe,

01:11:54.520 --> 01:11:57.800
it makes sense that you would feel this way.

01:11:57.800 --> 01:11:59.440
That person feels heard,

01:11:59.440 --> 01:12:01.280
and it allows you to validate someone

01:12:01.280 --> 01:12:03.720
that possibly is wrong, right?

01:12:03.720 --> 01:12:05.280
But we don't have to disagree with them,

01:12:05.280 --> 01:12:07.680
but we're able to maintain control of the call

01:12:07.680 --> 01:12:09.200
and then segue the solution.

01:12:09.200 --> 01:12:12.240
So it makes sense that you feel X, Y, and Z,

01:12:12.240 --> 01:12:14.160
and I'm glad that you called me for help.

01:12:14.160 --> 01:12:18.320
And what I wanna do next is you just completely segue

01:12:18.320 --> 01:12:20.960
into the solution to get control of the call.

01:12:20.960 --> 01:12:22.280
But if anyone wants to look it up,

01:12:22.280 --> 01:12:25.600
it's like a modified imago dialogue that they can apply.

01:12:25.600 --> 01:12:26.880
But I just have a question.

01:12:26.880 --> 01:12:28.240
What is the difference?

01:12:28.240 --> 01:12:29.920
Because to me, validating mirroring

01:12:29.920 --> 01:12:32.160
almost sounds like you're combining it.

01:12:32.160 --> 01:12:35.760
By just saying it makes sense, that's the validating part,

01:12:35.760 --> 01:12:38.480
but then the mirroring part is saying back to them

01:12:38.480 --> 01:12:40.640
whatever it is that they're frustrated about.

01:12:40.640 --> 01:12:43.000
So it's like a better mirroring, in my opinion.

01:12:43.000 --> 01:12:43.840
Yeah, yeah.

01:12:43.840 --> 01:12:46.000
So when you're validating-

01:12:46.000 --> 01:12:49.640
Rudy's texting that you're presenting in five minutes.

01:12:49.640 --> 01:12:50.640
I thought it was at-

01:12:51.640 --> 01:12:52.480
I thought it was running late.

01:12:52.480 --> 01:12:53.560
I thought it was 2.45.

01:12:53.560 --> 01:12:54.400
Can you ask her?

01:12:54.400 --> 01:12:55.240
Say, I get you downstairs.

01:12:55.240 --> 01:12:56.320
No, she's just trying to call-

01:13:09.480 --> 01:13:11.360
So we had a little bit of a scare

01:13:11.360 --> 01:13:13.480
where we were told that I spoke in five minutes,

01:13:13.480 --> 01:13:15.640
but it was really 20, so we were fine.

01:13:15.640 --> 01:13:19.960
We kinda remember where we left off, but we kinda don't.

01:13:20.160 --> 01:13:22.240
So this is not gonna be perfect,

01:13:22.240 --> 01:13:27.240
but I think we left off on having very specific sales.

01:13:29.080 --> 01:13:30.200
I know you don't like that.

01:13:30.200 --> 01:13:33.120
You don't call it sales, but very specific.

01:13:33.120 --> 01:13:33.960
What would you call it?

01:13:33.960 --> 01:13:34.800
Talking points?

01:13:34.800 --> 01:13:36.440
Yes, talking points.

01:13:36.440 --> 01:13:40.640
Talking points and how to kinda tie all that into,

01:13:42.280 --> 01:13:43.240
what was it that you said?

01:13:43.240 --> 01:13:44.600
Mirroring and validating.

01:13:44.600 --> 01:13:46.920
Validating, empathizing.

01:13:46.920 --> 01:13:47.760
Yeah.

01:13:48.120 --> 01:13:51.120
Can you just kinda maybe just tie it all in for us

01:13:51.120 --> 01:13:55.320
in terms of controlling the call?

01:13:55.320 --> 01:13:56.880
Yeah, absolutely.

01:13:56.880 --> 01:14:01.880
So often when a potential caller is calling a firm,

01:14:02.560 --> 01:14:07.280
it is very easy for that call to just derail.

01:14:07.280 --> 01:14:10.360
And often we'll see an intake specialist

01:14:10.360 --> 01:14:12.920
who needs call control training

01:14:12.920 --> 01:14:14.600
be on the phone for up to an hour.

01:14:14.600 --> 01:14:16.840
I am gonna be honest, that has happened to me.

01:14:17.600 --> 01:14:22.200
10 years ago in a firm, I'd feel terrible for the caller

01:14:22.200 --> 01:14:23.840
and I'd stay on the phone for an hour,

01:14:23.840 --> 01:14:25.080
but that's not helpful.

01:14:25.080 --> 01:14:27.920
And we're also not, as an intake specialist,

01:14:27.920 --> 01:14:30.040
you're not placing yourself in a place of authority.

01:14:30.040 --> 01:14:30.880
Yes.

01:14:30.880 --> 01:14:33.200
I always think of the doctor.

01:14:33.200 --> 01:14:35.040
When you go to the doctor's office,

01:14:35.040 --> 01:14:38.200
do they just let you ramble and talk about what you Googled

01:14:38.200 --> 01:14:40.080
and what you're gonna try and what you think you have?

01:14:40.080 --> 01:14:42.160
Or are they like, hold on.

01:14:42.160 --> 01:14:43.480
They ask you a bunch of questions.

01:14:43.480 --> 01:14:45.280
They're like, this is what we're gonna do to figure it out

01:14:45.280 --> 01:14:47.080
and then we'll come up with a game plan.

01:14:47.080 --> 01:14:48.120
Exactly. Right?

01:14:48.120 --> 01:14:48.960
Exactly.

01:14:48.960 --> 01:14:50.600
So they set the agenda.

01:14:50.600 --> 01:14:51.440
Yep.

01:14:51.440 --> 01:14:52.280
They set the agenda.

01:14:52.280 --> 01:14:53.120
I love that I'm gonna call it out going forward.

01:14:53.120 --> 01:14:53.960
Yes.

01:14:53.960 --> 01:14:54.920
This is what we're gonna do first.

01:14:54.920 --> 01:14:55.760
Yes.

01:14:55.760 --> 01:14:56.680
And then we're gonna do that.

01:14:56.680 --> 01:14:57.520
Yep.

01:14:57.520 --> 01:14:58.760
And that will lead you to this.

01:14:58.760 --> 01:14:59.600
Yep.

01:14:59.600 --> 01:15:01.520
So setting the agenda is crucial.

01:15:01.520 --> 01:15:04.800
And when emotions creep up into that call

01:15:04.800 --> 01:15:06.600
and start to derail the call,

01:15:06.600 --> 01:15:10.120
that is where you can use tools like empathizing with them.

01:15:10.120 --> 01:15:12.560
So I can imagine that you feel frustrated right now

01:15:12.560 --> 01:15:14.160
with X, Y, and Z.

01:15:14.160 --> 01:15:18.080
It makes sense that you're angry because X, Y, and Z.

01:15:18.080 --> 01:15:23.080
When someone is telling their story

01:15:23.120 --> 01:15:25.680
and they tend to be going through a loop,

01:15:25.680 --> 01:15:27.680
mirroring is very helpful

01:15:27.680 --> 01:15:30.080
because you're able to repeat back to them

01:15:30.080 --> 01:15:32.560
what they said in a very clear and concise way

01:15:32.560 --> 01:15:33.600
and ask them. And it feels hard.

01:15:33.600 --> 01:15:34.440
Yeah.

01:15:34.440 --> 01:15:35.280
Did I get that?

01:15:35.280 --> 01:15:36.480
Is there anything that I'm missing?

01:15:36.480 --> 01:15:37.320
Yeah.

01:15:37.320 --> 01:15:41.000
That way they understand that you heard them,

01:15:41.000 --> 01:15:43.080
that you understand what they're saying,

01:15:43.080 --> 01:15:45.760
and then you segue into the solution.

01:15:45.760 --> 01:15:47.320
Often when people are repeating themselves,

01:15:47.320 --> 01:15:51.800
it's because they feel like you're missing something.

01:15:51.800 --> 01:15:53.440
So that's very helpful in that case.

01:15:53.440 --> 01:15:56.440
Do you train them to say stuff like, it sounds like?

01:15:56.440 --> 01:15:59.960
I guess in a way that is part of validating them, right?

01:15:59.960 --> 01:16:02.000
Because we're saying to them,

01:16:02.000 --> 01:16:04.720
it makes sense that you feel this way, right?

01:16:04.720 --> 01:16:06.600
From what I understand,

01:16:06.600 --> 01:16:10.240
you'll experience X, Y, and Z and X, Y, and Z.

01:16:10.240 --> 01:16:11.080
Did I get that?

01:16:11.080 --> 01:16:11.920
Is that correct?

01:16:11.920 --> 01:16:12.760
Yeah.

01:16:13.440 --> 01:16:14.640
It gives them control to be able to say, yes,

01:16:14.640 --> 01:16:15.480
that's correct.

01:16:15.480 --> 01:16:16.400
You got everything.

01:16:16.400 --> 01:16:17.240
Thank you.

01:16:17.240 --> 01:16:18.080
They feel validated.

01:16:18.080 --> 01:16:19.720
And even if there may be,

01:16:20.600 --> 01:16:22.000
maybe if it's not warranted,

01:16:22.000 --> 01:16:26.640
just validating them helps to end and land the plane,

01:16:26.640 --> 01:16:30.000
especially if the emotional caller is maybe upset

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:33.160
over their case getting rejected, which is very common.

01:16:33.160 --> 01:16:34.880
We're doing this a little bit backwards,

01:16:34.880 --> 01:16:37.120
but I first wanted everyone to hear

01:16:37.120 --> 01:16:38.960
how much knowledge you have.

01:16:38.960 --> 01:16:40.760
But now I think we should back it up with like,

01:16:40.760 --> 01:16:41.880
how do you know all this?

01:16:42.080 --> 01:16:43.560
What is your background?

01:16:43.560 --> 01:16:46.080
So can you quickly just tell us,

01:16:46.080 --> 01:16:47.560
how did you learn about intake?

01:16:47.560 --> 01:16:49.640
And what have you been doing for the past?

01:16:49.640 --> 01:16:51.560
What is your experience basically?

01:16:51.560 --> 01:16:55.640
I know you just started Legal Intake Pros.

01:16:55.640 --> 01:16:56.640
Legal Intake Pros.

01:16:56.640 --> 01:16:57.480
I just think of you.

01:16:57.480 --> 01:16:59.680
I don't even think of the business.

01:16:59.680 --> 01:17:01.080
And I was torn.

01:17:01.080 --> 01:17:02.800
I'm not gonna lie about having you on

01:17:02.800 --> 01:17:05.120
because you're like a secret weapon

01:17:05.120 --> 01:17:07.880
that I use for my clients.

01:17:07.920 --> 01:17:12.920
But I just think it's like such valuable information.

01:17:13.280 --> 01:17:15.680
So give us a little bit of your background.

01:17:15.680 --> 01:17:19.360
Yeah, so my entire career, just to recap,

01:17:19.360 --> 01:17:23.160
has been focused on marketing and advertising,

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:26.160
specifically multicultural marketing and advertising

01:17:26.160 --> 01:17:27.440
in the Latino community.

01:17:27.440 --> 01:17:31.600
And that kind of segues somehow into the insurance industry.

01:17:31.600 --> 01:17:33.320
So I was an insurance agent for many years,

01:17:33.320 --> 01:17:37.000
writing car insurance policies, workers' comp policies,

01:17:37.040 --> 01:17:38.240
commercial policies.

01:17:38.240 --> 01:17:40.920
Yeah, and I oversaw a sales team.

01:17:40.920 --> 01:17:42.520
That is where I got my sales experience.

01:17:42.520 --> 01:17:43.360
Got it, okay.

01:17:43.360 --> 01:17:45.040
That is where I really had an understanding

01:17:45.040 --> 01:17:47.560
of property and casualty and insurance.

01:17:47.560 --> 01:17:49.720
And interesting enough,

01:17:49.720 --> 01:17:53.160
that led me into the personal injury space.

01:17:53.160 --> 01:17:55.440
And I was working as an intake director

01:17:55.440 --> 01:17:58.640
and a marketing director for a high volume firm

01:17:58.640 --> 01:18:01.480
that practiced primarily workers' compensation

01:18:01.480 --> 01:18:04.240
and personal injury cases.

01:18:04.280 --> 01:18:07.920
And it was a fast growing firm.

01:18:07.920 --> 01:18:10.160
I had a lot of pressure from all of the partners

01:18:10.160 --> 01:18:13.200
