01:20:03.860 --> 01:20:04.940
Calls are being missed.

01:20:04.940 --> 01:20:08.100
Like, I just think that's not the right mindset

01:20:08.100 --> 01:20:10.460
to be handling these intakes, right?

01:20:10.460 --> 01:20:11.940
Do you see that as a problem too,

01:20:11.940 --> 01:20:15.300
like understaffed intake teams?

01:20:15.300 --> 01:20:19.260
Yes, but I do think that the way that we measure

01:20:19.260 --> 01:20:21.620
when we need to expand the team

01:20:21.620 --> 01:20:23.820
should come after optimizing.

01:20:23.820 --> 01:20:24.820
So- I love that.

01:20:24.820 --> 01:20:25.940
That makes perfect sense.

01:20:25.940 --> 01:20:28.260
Remove all, find a way to automate,

01:20:28.260 --> 01:20:30.420
find a way to remove all the manual labor

01:20:30.420 --> 01:20:32.500
where you can, right?

01:20:32.500 --> 01:20:33.900
Remove the friction internally,

01:20:33.900 --> 01:20:36.940
remove the friction for the potential clients.

01:20:36.940 --> 01:20:38.420
And then let's reassess

01:20:38.420 --> 01:20:40.980
if this is more so an operational issue

01:20:40.980 --> 01:20:42.580
in terms of our processes,

01:20:42.580 --> 01:20:44.820
or if we need to expand our team.

01:20:44.820 --> 01:20:48.580
If we've optimized the intake team and our intake processes,

01:20:48.580 --> 01:20:49.980
and we are at the point now

01:20:49.980 --> 01:20:51.900
where our response time is suffering,

01:20:51.900 --> 01:20:53.180
that's a really good sign.

01:20:53.180 --> 01:20:55.660
Let's scale up, let's hire someone else on the team.

01:20:55.700 --> 01:21:00.140
But if we're choosing to keep the team at that size,

01:21:00.140 --> 01:21:02.300
then it's gonna cost cases, obviously.

